 {"index":345,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Wolfe: characterize by showing thinking, speaking, acting characteristically.","note":"Stewart's blog (POSSIBLY IRRITATING ESSAYS, Feb 2022) relays five bits of advice Wolfe gave Neil Gaiman; the quote sits verbatim in the fourth: 'You do not characterize by telling the reader about the character. You do it by showing the character thinking, speaking and acting in a characteristic way. You simply show it and shut up.' Every limb holds, including 'rather than described'."},
 {"index":346,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Ambiguity: organising principle of form and fundamental theme.","note":"Opening paragraph, verbatim: 'ambiguity is the watchword to the text. More significantly, it is also an organising principle of form, a means of confounding interpretation, and a fundamental theme...'. Article by Peter Wright, Ultan's Library, 15 January 2002; the 'not only a theme' limb is supported because the page asserts both roles."},
 {"index":347,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Puzzle's existence more significant than its solution.","note":"Verbatim (including the page's own typo 'more significant that its solution'), and located exactly where the claim says - the paragraph closing the Wendland discussion: '...the existence of the puzzle is more significant that its solution, since the puzzle is where the political arguments of the novel can be found.'"},
 {"index":348,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Early-1980s readers accused Wolfe of fabricating words; untrue.","note":"Wowra's introduction to his 2009 interview with Andre-Driussi: 'In the early 1980s, frustrated readers accused Mr. Wolfe of deliberately fabricating unusual words to confuse them. Nothing could be further from the truth.' Both limbs (the early-1980s dating and the denial) are on the page."},
 {"index":349,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Real strange words show how odd language sounds.","note":"Verbatim: 'All of the strange words that appear in THE BOOK OF THE NEW SUN are real. And they remind us just how odd language can sound without science fiction authors inventing new words that lack inherent meaning.' The 'without any invented vocabulary' limb is the page's own clause."},
