 {"index":351,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Range actually one thousand to thirty-thousand years, revising 'about a chiliad'.","note":"Section 2.1 Overview, verbatim: 'In “Posthistory 101” and Lexicon Urthus I stated that the time from Ymar to Severian was about a chiliad. Recent reading suggests that the range is actually between one thousand and thirty-thousand years...'. By Michael Andre-Driussi, Ultan's Library, 6 July 2020; the revision limb is explicit."},
 {"index":352,"verdict":"PARTIAL","unsupportedLimb":"that the earlier thousand-year figure rested on a single character's remark - the page names a second basis, Wolfe's own remark in a 1983 Robert Frazier interview ('an autarch a chiliad dead'), and concludes 'Based, then, on Vodalus’ and Wolfe’s comments, I took the 1K model as being solid'","trueWording":"Vodalus's line plus Wolfe's 1983 interview remark grounded 1K.","note":"Quote is verbatim in section 2.2 ('This statement strongly implied to me that the autarchs had reigned for about a thousand years'), and the 'rather than any stated chronology' limb is supported ('it turns out I had not adequately cited the source text for that detail'). The 'single character's offhand remark' limb overstates: the source text is Vodalus's recruitment-pitch line (II, chap. 10) AND Wolfe's interview statement, which Andre-Driussi says he was 'perhaps unduly influenced by'."},
 {"index":353,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Brute-force search for chiliads, thousand years, ages, aeons, eons.","note":"Section 2.3 Narrowing the range, verbatim: 'In an attempt to narrow that down a bit, I set upon a brute force method of searching for the terms “chiliads,” “thousand years,” “ages,” “aeons,” and “eons,” in order to sift for further clues.' The five time words match the claim exactly, and the narrowing purpose is stated."},
 {"index":354,"verdict":"PARTIAL","unsupportedLimb":"that this advice came in a letter - the memoir introduces it only as 'He once advised', naming no setting; the letter it does describe is the earlier critique of her novel chapters (the 'uncooked dumplings' letter)","trueWording":"'He once advised' - setting unnamed, not attributed to a letter.","note":"Quotation verbatim: 'He once advised, “Always tell a story as cleanly and as clearly as possible.”' Cooney's memoir (Ultan's Library, 10 June 2016) mixes dinner conversation and correspondence, and this line is not placed in either. Secondary: the page calls her a poet and short-story writer with an unsold novel; 'novelist' is not the page's word."},
 {"index":355,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"Letter on her chapters: infodumps, 'lumps of prose like uncooked dumplings'.","note":"Verbatim and every limb holds: 'he wrote me a long letter about what he did read... I had a tendency to interrupt my dialogue with long infodumps. He used the phrase “lumps of prose like uncooked dumplings”'. The draft was the first chapters of her novel, which she had sent him."},
 {"index":356,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"She'd cut sing-songy rhythm and rhyme; he urged otherwise.","note":"Verbatim, and directly after the clean-and-clear advice as the claim locates it: 'after I wrote “Three Fancies from the Infernal Garden”... and showed it to Gene, telling him I’d probably need to cut much of the sing-songy rhythm and internal rhyme, he urged me not to take my knife to it.'"},
 {"index":357,"verdict":V,"trueWording":"5HoC's opening line riffs on Proust's 'For a long time I went to bed early.'","note":"Verbatim: 'The opening line of The Fifth Head of Cerberus... is a riff on Proust’s “For a long time I went to bed early.”' By Matthew Keeley, Tor.com/Reactor, published 9 November 2017 (datePublished 2017-11-09). The page names Swann's Way as the work in the next sentence ('Readers unfamiliar with Swann’s Way... people who recognize the source quote'); it does not itself use the word 'opening' for the Proust line, though that line is Swann's Way's first sentence."},
